.SH "6\&.21\&.0"
.PP
.SS "Minor Changes"
.IP "\(bu" 2
Add a new \fRfuture\&.v7_relativeSplatPath\fP flag to implement a breaking bug fix to relative routing when inside a splat route\&. (\fR#11087\fP)

.PP
This fix was originally added in \fR#10983\fP and was later reverted in \fR#11078\fP because it was determined that a large number of existing applications were relying on the buggy behavior (see \fR#11052\fP)

.PP
\fBThe Bug\fP The buggy behavior is that without this flag, the default behavior when resolving relative paths is to \fIignore\fP any splat (\fR*\fP) portion of the current route path\&.

.PP
\fBThe Background\fP This decision was originally made thinking that it would make the concept of nested different sections of your apps in \fR<Routes>\fP easier if relative routing would \fIreplace\fP the current splat:

.PP
.PP
.nf
<BrowserRouter>
  <Routes>
    <Route path="/" element={<Home />} />
    <Route path="dashboard/*" element={<Dashboard />} />
  </Routes>
</BrowserRouter>
.fi
.PP

.PP
Any paths like \fR/dashboard\fP, \fR/dashboard/team\fP, \fR/dashboard/projects\fP will match the \fRDashboard\fP route\&. The dashboard component itself can then render nested \fR<Routes>\fP:

.PP
.PP
.nf
function Dashboard() {
  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Dashboard</h2>
      <nav>
        <Link to="/">Dashboard Home</Link>
        <Link to="team">Team</Link>
        <Link to="projects">Projects</Link>
      </nav>

      <Routes>
        <Route path="/" element={<DashboardHome />} />
        <Route path="team" element={<DashboardTeam />} />
        <Route path="projects" element={<DashboardProjects />} />
      </Routes>
    </div>
  );
}
.fi
.PP

.PP
Now, all links and route paths are relative to the router above them\&. This makes code splitting and compartmentalizing your app really easy\&. You could render the \fRDashboard\fP as its own independent app, or embed it into your large app without making any changes to it\&.

.PP
\fBThe Problem\fP

.PP
The problem is that this concept of ignoring part of a path breaks a lot of other assumptions in React Router - namely that \fR"\&."\fP always means the current location pathname for that route\&. When we ignore the splat portion, we start getting invalid paths when using \fR"\&."\fP:

.PP
.PP
.nf
// If we are on URL /dashboard/team, and we want to link to /dashboard/team:
function DashboardTeam() {
  // ❌ This is broken and results in <a href="/dashboard">
  return <Link to="\&.">A broken link to the Current URL</Link>;

  // ✅ This is fixed but super unintuitive since we're already at /dashboard/team!
  return <Link to="\&./team">A broken link to the Current URL</Link>;
}
.fi
.PP

.PP
We've also introduced an issue that we can no longer move our \fRDashboardTeam\fP component around our route hierarchy easily - since it behaves differently if we're underneath a non-splat route, such as \fR/dashboard/:widget\fP\&. Now, our \fR"\&."\fP links will, properly point to ourself \fIinclusive of the dynamic param value\fP so behavior will break from it's corresponding usage in a \fR/dashboard/*\fP route\&.

.PP
Even worse, consider a nested splat route configuration:

.PP
.PP
.nf
<BrowserRouter>
  <Routes>
    <Route path="dashboard">
      <Route path="*" element={<Dashboard />} />
    </Route>
  </Routes>
</BrowserRouter>
.fi
.PP

.PP
Now, a \fR<Link to="\&.">\fP and a \fR<Link to="\&.\&.">\fP inside the \fRDashboard\fP component go to the same place! That is definitely not correct!

.PP
Another common issue arose in Data Routers (and Remix) where any \fR<Form>\fP should post to it's own route \fRaction\fP if you the user doesn't specify a form action:

.PP
.PP
.nf
let router = createBrowserRouter({
  path: "/dashboard",
  children: [
    {
      path: "*",
      action: dashboardAction,
      Component() {
        // ❌ This form is broken!  It throws a 405 error when it submits because
        // it tries to submit to /dashboard (without the splat value) and the parent
        // `/dashboard` route doesn't have an action
        return <Form method="post">\&.\&.\&.</Form>;
      },
    },
  ],
});
.fi
.PP

.PP
This is just a compounded issue from the above because the default location for a \fRForm\fP to submit to is itself (\fR"\&."\fP) - and if we ignore the splat portion, that now resolves to the parent route\&.

.PP
\fBThe Solution\fP If you are leveraging this behavior, it's recommended to enable the future flag, move your splat to it's own route, and leverage \fR\&.\&./\fP for any links to "sibling" pages:

.PP
.PP
.nf
<BrowserRouter>
  <Routes>
    <Route path="dashboard">
      <Route index path="*" element={<Dashboard />} />
    </Route>
  </Routes>
</BrowserRouter>

function Dashboard() {
  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Dashboard</h2>
      <nav>
        <Link to="\&.\&.">Dashboard Home</Link>
        <Link to="\&.\&./team">Team</Link>
        <Link to="\&.\&./projects">Projects</Link>
      </nav>

      <Routes>
        <Route path="/" element={<DashboardHome />} />
        <Route path="team" element={<DashboardTeam />} />
        <Route path="projects" element={<DashboardProjects />} />
      </Router>
    </div>
  );
}
.fi
.PP

.PP
This way, \fR\&.\fP means "the full current pathname for my route" in all cases (including static, dynamic, and splat routes) and \fR\&.\&.\fP always means "my parents pathname"\&.
.PP
